  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 140 AM MDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nAs of 1:30AM, storms are moving through our southeastern \ncounties. The last of our discreet supercells, which likely \nproduced some large hail tonight, are moving east out of Lincoln \nCounty. Behind these storms, we have a line of weaker storms that \nhave developed along what I assume is an outflow boundary. The \nsouthern portion of Elbert County and middle/northern portion of \nLincoln county have been worked over by the round of storms prior \nto this line, but as the boundary continues to progress southeast \ninto southern Lincoln county, the storms could start to tap into \nsome better instability. We do have an isolated storm going up in \nsouthern lincoln currently (1:30AM) and it's staying below severe \nthresholds, so hopefully the line will follow suit and strengthen \nslightly, but remain sub severe as it moves southeast. Given the \ncurrent storm motion, the line of storms should move out of our \nforecast area around 3:30AM. \n\nNear normal temperates are expected this afternoon, with highs in \nthe mid 80s to around 90 degrees across the plains. Flow aloft will \nturn more to the northwest as upper level ridging builds over the \nfour corners region. There will be a weak shortwave that moves \nthrough the flow aloft, but models are showing it to the south of our \narea, giving PUBs forecast area the higher rain coverage for this \nafternoon. Some weak upslope from the easterly surface winds could be \nenough to trigger some isolated to scattered activity near the higher \nterrain, but coverage will be best in Park county and the Palmer \nDivide area for our CWA. Shear will be strong once again today, \nbut MLCAPEs will generally remain less than 800 J/kg this\nafternoon, limiting our severe threat. With that being said, \nDCAPEs could be in excess of 1000 J/kg today, so we could still an\nisolated strong wind threat.\n\nThe ridge will continue to build Thursday and Friday, increasing \ntemperatures to above normal. Highs are forecast to peak on \nFriday, with temperatures in the plains getting into the mid to \nupper 90s. PWs in the high country are forecast to stay around \n100% to 140% of normal during this time frame. So, we could still\nsee some isolated activity Thursday and Friday afternoon, but \ncoverage will likely stay confined to the higher terrain. \n\nThe upper level ridging will start to push east on Saturday, turning \nwinds aloft to the southwest. At the surface a weak boundary will\nmove through, turning winds more easterly for Saturday. Because of\nthese two things, we'll see an influx of moisture for Saturday \ninto Sunday, bringing PWs back into the 130% to 200% range for our\nCWA. Temperatures will lower slightly for the weekend, but remain\nabove normal through the start of next week. Starting Saturday, \nmodels start to diverge on the eastward extent of rain chances. \nMoisture will be present, so it really just depends on the timing \nof potential lifting mechanisms (disturbances \naloft/upslope/boundaries/etc). Right now, ensemble members are \npretty all over the place for next week, with around 30-40% of \nmembers showing measurable rain making it out of the higher \nterrain and onto the plains each afternoon.",
